islikes roti, toast. ', 'menu_items': ['toast', 'bread', 'doughnut', 'roti'], 'gpt_out': "Let's begin by listing the likes and dislikes as sets for each individual to have a clearer understanding:\n\n- Joy: Likes {bread}, Dislikes {doughnut, roti}\n- Joe: Likes {roti, bread}, Dislikes {doughnut}\n- Lia: Likes {bread, doughnut}, Dislikes {toast}\n- Ivy: Likes {bread}, Dislikes {doughnut, toast}\n- Lei: Likes {toast}, Dislikes {bread, doughnut}\n- Sia: Likes {doughnut, bread, roti}, Dislikes {}\n- Sam: Likes {bread, roti}, Dislikes {doughnut}\n- Cal: Likes {bread, roti, doughnut}, Dislikes {}\n- Liv: Likes {doughnut, bread}, Dislikes {roti}\n- Jaya: Likes {toast}, Dislikes {bread, doughnut}\n- Chao: Likes {doughnut, bread}, Dislikes {roti}\n- Geet: Likes {bread}, Dislikes {roti, doughnut}\n- Jim: Likes {toast, doughnut}, Dislikes {roti}\n- Pia: Likes {toast, bread}, Dislikes {doughnut}\n- Ansh: Likes {toast, roti}, Dislikes {doughnut}\n- Arun: Likes {bread, toast, doughnut}, Dislikes {}\n- Ian: Likes {roti, doughnut}, Dislikes {toast}\n- Isha: Likes {toast}, Dislikes {roti, doughnut}\n- Raj: Likes {bread}, Dislikes {roti, doughnut}\n- Rohan: Likes {}, Dislikes {bread, toast, roti}\n- Oli: Likes {roti, doughnut}, Dislikes {bread}\n- Ana: Likes {toast, bread}, Dislikes {roti}\n- Kai: Likes {roti, bread}, Dislikes {toast}\n- Nic: Likes {}, Dislikes {doughnut, toast, roti}\n- Bea: Likes {roti, doughnut}, Dislikes {bread}\n- Anil: Likes {}, Dislikes {roti, doughnut, toast}\n- Eva: Likes {bread, roti}, Dislikes {toast}\n- Ned: Likes {doughnut, bread}, Dislikes {roti}\n- Tim: Likes {doughnut, roti}, Dislikes {bread}\n- Kim: Likes {roti, bread}, Dislikes {doughnut}\n- Gia: Likes {toast, roti}, Dislikes {doughnut}\n- Wes: Likes {toast}, Dislikes {roti, bread}\n- Leo: Likes {bread, roti, toast}, Dislikes {}\n- Mia: Likes {roti, bread}, Dislikes {toast}\n- Zac: Likes {bread, doughnut}, Dislikes {roti}\n- Taj: Likes {bread}, Dislikes {roti, toast}\n\nFrom these sets, let's try to find items for the 'orderable' (O) and 'not_orderable' (NO) lists that would make every individual satisfied according to the rules:\n\n1. An item that is disliked by one person and liked by none should be in NO.\n2. An item that is liked by at least one person and not disliked by any others should be in O.\n3. If an item is liked by some and disliked by others, we need to make a decision based on whom we can satisfy with other items.\n\nLet's begin with rule 1:\n- Anil, Rohan, and Nic all have dislikes but no likes, meaning their dislikes must be in NO to satisfy them.\n\nFrom Anil: {roti, doughnut, toast} must all go in NO.\nFrom Rohan: {bread, toast, roti} intersect with Anil's dislikes, confirm {bread} must go in NO.\nFrom Nic: {doughnut, toast, roti} intersect with the above, no new information.\n\nAt this point, we already have a problem: Anil's and Rohan's dislikes mean both 'toast' and 'bread' should be in NO, but there are individuals who only like 'toast' or 'bread' and dislike 'doughnut', such as Lei, Jaya, and Isha, which would mean 'doughnut' should also be in NO to satisfy them. However, since everyone must be satisfied and 'doughnut' cannot be in both O and NO, we cannot find a satisfactory configuration for both of these items ('toast', 'bread') and 'doughnut'.\n\nThus, no arrangement exists that can satisfy all individuals simultaneously with the given criteria.\n\nSo, both our lists are:\n\norderable=[]\nnot_orderable=[]", 'num_prompt_tokens': 647, 'num_completion_tokens': 979}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[-1, -3, 5], [6, 5, -4], [4, 3, 1], [-4, -6, 5], [-3, -1, 6], [3, 2, 1], [2, 5, -6], [-3, 1, -5], [6, 3, -2], [5, 6, 3], [1, 6, -2], [-5, -1, -4], [-2, 5, -1], [2, 1, -4], [-4, 6, 5], [5, -6, -3], [-4, 6, -1], [2, -1, 6], [5, -6, -4], [-6, 3, 5], [-1, -5, 4], [-5, -4, -3], [6, -2, 3], [2, 3, -4]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Rob: Likes kimchi. Dislikes shawarma, ramen. Jon: Likes toast, kimchi. Dislikes couscous. Dan: Likes couscous, ramen, shawarma. Tao: Likes kimchi. Dislikes couscous, toast. Sal: Likes toast. Dislikes ramen, shawarma. Zoe: Likes ramen, poke, shawarma. Zan: Likes poke, kimchi. Dislikes toast. Hu: Likes shawarma. Dislikes ramen, kimchi. Gia: Likes toast, ramen. Dislikes poke. Cy: Likes kimchi, toast, ramen. Ula: Likes shawarma, toast. Dislikes poke. Bea: Dislikes kimchi, shawarma, couscous. Tom: Likes kimchi. Dislikes poke, shawarma. Diya: Likes poke, shawarma. Dislikes couscous. Sol: Likes toast, kimchi. Dislikes couscous. Ray: Likes kimchi. Dislikes toast, ramen. Bo: Likes toast. Dislikes couscous, shawarma. Ben: Likes poke, toast. Dislikes shawarma. Sue: Likes kimchi. Dislikes toast, couscous. Arun: Likes ramen, kimchi. Dislikes toast. Ty: Likes couscous. Dislikes shawarma, kimchi. Ansh: Dislikes kimchi, couscous, ramen. Yan: Likes toast, ramen. Dislikes poke. Du: Likes poke, ramen. Dislikes couscous. ', 'menu_items': ['shawarma', 'poke', 'ramen', 'couscous', 'kimchi', 'toast'], 'gpt_out': "Given the preferences of the group, we need to find at least one liked food item for each individual to be on the 'orderable' list, or at least one disliked food item for each individual to be on the 'not_orderable' list, without any item appearing on both lists. Let's go through the preferences to determine the choices:\n\nLikes and Dislikes of the Group:\n\nKimchi: \nLikes: Rob, Jon, Tao, Zan, Cy, Tom, Sol, Sue, Ray, Arun\nDislikes: Hu, Bea, Ty, Ansh\n(1 like and 1 dislike - neutral, but more likes than dislikes)\n\nToast:\nLikes: Jon, Sal, Gia, Cy, Ula, Sol, Bo, Ben, Ray, Yan\nDislikes: Tao, Zan, Sue, Ray\n(10 likes and 4 dislikes - positive, can be orderable)\n\nCouscous:\nLikes: Dan, Ty\nDislikes: Jon, Tao, Bea, Diya, Sol, Bo, Ansh, Du\n(2 likes and 8 dislikes - negative, can be not_orderable)\n\nRamen:\nLikes: Dan, Zoe, Gia, Cy, Arun, Yan, Du\nDislikes: Rob, Sal, Hu, Ray, Ansh\n(7 likes and 5 dislikes - positive, can be orderable)\n\nShawarma:\nLikes: Dan, Zoe, Hu, Ula, Diya\nDislikes: Rob, Sal, Bea, Ty, Bo, Tom, Ben\n(5 likes and 7 dislikes - negative, can be not_orderable)\n\nPoke:\nLikes: Zoe, Zan, Diya, Ben, Du\nDislikes: Gia, Ula, Bea, Tom, Yan, Ansh\n(5 likes and 6 dislikes - negative, can be not_orderable)\n\nNow, let's check if the lists we've created can satisfy everyone in the group:\n\n1. For those who like toast and ramen, having them in the 'orderable' list should be satisfactory.\n2. For those who dislike couscous, shawarma, and poke, having them in the 'not_orderable' list should be satisfactory.\n\nThe combination that seems to satisfy everyone would place toast and ramen on 'orderable' and couscous, shawarma, and poke on 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's confirm that this combination works for each individual:\n\n- Rob: Satisfied with ramen being orderable or shawarma not orderable\n- Jon: Satisfied with toast being orderable\n- Dan: Satisfied with ramen being orderable\n- Tao: Satisfied with toast being orderable or couscous not orderable\n- Sal: Satisfied with toast being orderable\n- Zoe: Satisfied with ramen being orderable\n- Zan: Satisfied with ramen being orderable\n- Hu: Satisfied with shawarma not orderable\n- Gia: Satisfied with toast and ramen being orderable\n- Cy: Satisfied with toast and ramen being orderable\n- Ula: Satisfied with toast being orderable\n- Bea: Satisfied with couscous and shawarma being not orderable\n- Tom: Satisfied with shawarma being not orderable\n- Diya: Satisfied with shawarma or poke being not orderable\n- Sol: Satisfied with toast being orderable\n- Ray: Satisfied with shawarma being not orderable\n- Bo: Satisfied with toast being orderable\n- Ben: Satisfied with poke being not orderable\n- Sue: Satisfied with shawarma being not orderable\n- Arun: Satisfied with ramen being orderable\n- Ty: Satisfied with shawarma being not orderable\n- Ansh: Satisfied with all dislikes being not orderable\n- Yan: Satisfied with toast and ramen being orderable\n- Du: Satisfied with ramen being orderable\n\nGiven that all individuals in the group will be satisfied by the combination of these two lists, where toast and ramen are orderable, and couscous, shawarma, and poke are not, we can confidently write out our result:\n\n```python\norderable = ['toast', 'ramen']\nnot_orderable = ['couscous', 'shawarma', 'poke']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 526, 'num_completion_tokens': 914}
