ike items that nobody else likes; these items can go straight into the \'not_orderable\' list.\n2. Next, look for individuals who like items that nobody else dislikes; these items can go into the \'orderable\' list.\n3. Check for conflicts, where an individual\'s liking and another\'s disliking overlap; we must ensure no item ends up on both lists.\n\nLet\'s analyze the given data:\n\n- Items that are only disliked and not liked by anyone else should go into the \'not_orderable\' list. In this case, we don\'t have any such item because every disliked item is also liked by at least one person.\n\n- Items that are only liked and not disliked by anyone else should go into the \'orderable\' list. No such item exists here, as all liked items are also disliked by someone.\n\nWe need to find a satisfactory compromise. Let\'s start:\n\n- Everyone who dislikes "burger" also likes something else. Since "burger" is not universally disliked and some individuals have expressed a liking for it, we cannot include it in either list at this moment.\n\nNow, let\'s try to find the orderable items that satisfy the most people without offending others:\n\n- There are several likes for "omelette", "tandoori", "waffles", "nachos", and "tacos", but they are also disliked by some.\n\n- "Paneer" is liked by some, but it\'s also disliked by multiple individuals.\n\nWe can\'t select "burger" since people both like and dislike it, so we have to satisfy those individuals with other items.\n\nLet\'s satisfy people based on their dislikes, trying to minimize conflicts:\n\n- "Veer" dislikes "waffles", "paneer", and "tacos". We can\'t satisfy him by dislikes, because there\'s no item that he dislikes that isn\'t liked by many others. So we need to ensure that "omelette", "tandoori", "nachos" or "burger" will be orderable.\n\nHere\'s the plan based on reducing conflicts:\n\n1. Since "tandoori" is disliked by only one person (Lee), and liked by many, we can tentatively add it to the \'orderable\' list.\n\n2. Adding "tandoori" to \'orderable\' satisfies Rohan, Ping, Dan, Lan, Ravi, Lex, Meg, Aarav, and Abe all at once, because they all dislike items that we can\'t safely put on either list due to multiple conflicts.\n\n3. "Nachos" are liked by many and only disliked by one person (Lex). Thus, we can add "nachos" to the \'orderable\' list as well. This satisfies Lee (through "tandoori"), Ty, Tao, Tara, Cal, Gus, Ru, Shan, Ada, Jaya, Rob, and everyone satisfied by "tandoori".\n\n4. "Waffles" can now be placed in \'not_orderable\'. This is because the only people for whom "waffles" are the sole liked item have already been satisfied by "tandoori" or "nachos".\n\n5. "Paneer", "omelette", and "tacos" cannot be in \'orderable\' because they are disliked by multiple people. "Burger" cannot be in \'not_orderable\' because it\'s liked by multiple individuals as well.\n\nNow our preliminary lists are:\n- orderable: tandoori, nachos\n- not_orderable: waffles\n\nFinally, we check if everyone is satisfied:\n\n- Fay is satisfied by \'not_orderable\' ("waffles") or \'orderable\' ("tandoori" or "nachos").\n- All others are satisfied through "tandoori", "nachos", or \'not_orderable\' ("waffles").\n\nSince no item is on both lists and everyone\'s preferences have been met with the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' items, our solution satisfies all the given constraints.\n\nThe final solution is:\n```python\norderable=[\'tandoori\', \'nachos\'], not_orderable=[\'waffles\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 588, 'num_completion_tokens': 888}
