 Veer, Fay, Tim, Geet, and Joy) have other interests that are also contentious, so wonton alone can't be considered 'not_orderable'.\n- Individuals who dislike fries (Lan, Om, Eva, Tim, Pam, Ru, and Liz) all have other likes or dislikes, making fries an unsuitable candidate for the 'orderable' list.\n\nGiven the complexity, let's reassess our approach:\n\nSince caramel can generally satisfy those who like it (there is a compensating item for everyone who dislikes it), we place caramel in 'orderable'. Anyone satisfied by caramel being available can be disregarded in further considerations.\n\nNow, let's try to find an item that can be designated 'not_orderable'. We look at the dislikes of people whose likes have already been satisfied with caramel being 'orderable':\n\n- For all those satisfied by caramel, their dislikes are wonton, biscuits, and fries. Out of these, the least liked by others is biscuits. However, since Lan and Zac dislike every item, we cannot satisfy them by not ordering something they dislike, so we will have to make sure there is something they like on the 'orderable' list. \n\nSo we will also add fries to the 'orderable' list to satisfy people who are already satisfied by caramel or fries being available, leaving wonton and biscuits as potentially 'not_orderable' due to them being disliked by several who would be already satisfied.\n\nChecking for biscuits, everyone who likes biscuits is also satisfied by either caramel or fries being available. Since these individuals are already satisfied with the 'orderable' items, we can add biscuits to 'not_orderable'.\n\nSince fries, caramel, and biscuits are either 'orderable' or 'not_orderable', we don't need to consider them further.\n\nFor wonton, every person that likes wonton also likes one of the 'orderable' items caramel and fries, meaning we can add it to 'not_orderable', and now we have a solution that satisfies everyone.\n\n'Orderable' List: caramel, fries\n'Not_orderable' List: biscuits, wonton