d – likes "salad," which is not widely disliked, so "salad" can also be in \'orderable\'.\n- Min and Li need "peach" replaced – no further likes to consider for them, as they like only one item each.\n  \nAt this point, we\'ve placed "couscous" and "salad" into \'orderable\' to satisfy Wei and Wes:\n\n- orderable = [\'couscous\', \'salad\']\n\nNext, let\'s address "kebab". Individuals who like "kebab" (Cy, Abe, Jim, Yan, Riya) have "kebab" as their only like, except for Jim who also likes "couscous" which is already in \'orderable\'. "kebab" is not a dislike exclusive to Hal, as there are others who dislike it too. So "kebab" cannot be in \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' just yet.\n\nWe have yet to satisfy Hal, May, Chen, Meg, Aarav, Cy, Raj, Abe, Kavi, Yan, Lee, Gen, Jim, Bea, and Zara. A potential issue arises with individuals like Hal, who dislikes everything Veer likes, making it impossible to satisfy both with a shared item.\n\nConsidering these constraints, there is no combination that satisfies everyone because the dislikes of some are the only likes of others. For instance, Hal dislikes "doughnut", "salad", and "kebab", which are the only items liked by Meg, Aarav, Cy, and Kavi. Thus, we can\'t find a satisfactory \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' arrangement.\n\nTherefore:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 521, 'num_completion_tokens': 1099}
