s, shawarma, pizza, mousse\n\nDisliked by at least one person:\n- shrimps\n- vegetables\n- shawarma\n- mousse\n- pizza (only Liv and Oli dislike pizza, so this will be challenging to place in the 'not_orderable' list as it's also widely liked.)\n\nShawarma is disliked by many, and it's hard to find someone who likes shawarma but does not like anything else. Hence, we should consider adding shawarma to the 'not_orderable' list first. However, before we do so, we must ensure at least one liked item for everyone who likes shawarma is in the 'orderable' list.\n\nWe notice that almost all individuals who like shawarma also like pizza, except for Liv, who dislikes pizza and Sol, who also dislikes pizza but likes shrimps and mousse. Therefore, to satisfy Liv if we put shrimps in 'orderable', we need to make sure that those who dislike shrimps have something they like in 'orderable' or dislike in 'not_orderable'.\n\nParticipants who dislike shrimps like pizza, vegetables, or mousse. Since almost everyone likes pizza, and most shrimps dislikers also like at least one of the other two, including shrimps in 'orderable' is safe.\n\nLou dislikes shawarma, Lou dislikes pizza too but likes mousse; therefore, we also need mousse to be on the 'orderable' list to satisfy Lou if shawarma is 'not_orderable'. We need to check if we can place mousse to 'orderable' without conflict. The only problem arises with Liv, who dislikes pizza and mousse, but we're placing shrimps in 'orderable', which satisfies Liv.\n\nAll the individuals who dislike mousse either like pizza or shrimps or both. So, we can add mousse to the 'orderable' list too.\n\nLastly, we must add pizza to the 'orderable' list to satisfy a vast majority of individuals, except those who specifically dislike pizza (Liv and Oli), but Liv is already satisfied with shrimps, and Oli likes shrimps as well.\n\nBased on this analysis, we can organize the foods:\n\n- Pizza should be placed in 'orderable' because it is liked by many and only dislikes by Liv and Oli, which we have mitigated with shrimps.\n- Shrimps should be placed in 'orderable' because it satisfies Liv, who dislikes both pizza and mousse, and also Oli is satisfied with this inclusion.\n- Mousse should be placed in 'orderable' because Lou, who dislikes both shawarma and pizza, likes mousse.\n- Shawarma should be placed in 'not_orderable' since we have made sure everyone liking shawarma will be satisfied with other food items.\n- Vegetables are liked and disliked by some, but since their preference is not impacting the satisfaction (as other items cover their likes), we can ignore them. \n\nNow we must ensure that every participant is satisfied:\n\n- Every individual who likes shawarma finds satisfaction in pizza, shrimps, or mousse, which are 'orderable.'\n- Everyone who dislikes shawarma is satisfied as it’s 'not_orderable.'\n- Everyone who likes pizza is satisfied because it's 'orderable.'\n- Everyone who likes shrimps is satisfied.\n- Everyone who likes mousse is satisfied.\n\nNo contradictions are found, so the list remains as predicted:\n\n```python\norderable=['pizza', 'shrimps', 'mousse'], not_orderable=['shawarma']\n``` \n\nVegetables are not mentioned in either list because their placement is unnecessary to achieve satisfaction, as everyone who likes vegetables also likes at least one of the 'orderable' items and anyone who dislikes vegetables will not find them on either list, which adheres to the criteria.", 'num_prompt_tokens': 969, 'num_completion_tokens': 932}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 10, 'num_clauses': 20, 'formula': [[1, -8, 3], [-9, 10, -7], [3, -8, 2], [6, -3, 7], [-4, -10, -8], [8, 1, -2], [9, 10, 1], [-10, 5, 1], [1, -9, 6], [4, -7, -9], [-2, -5, 8], [-3, -4, -2], [-8, -1, -6], [5, -8, 3], [4, 7, -6], [-9, 8, -2], [-1, -6, 5], [6, 5, -8], [3, -7, 1], [-1, 4, -10]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Veer: Likes kebab, doughnut. Dislikes custard. Ken: Likes shawarma. Dislikes sorbet, muffins. Vic: Likes doughnut, toast. Dislikes custard. Eli: Likes pizza, muffins. Dislikes doughnut. Anil: Dislikes risotto, shawarma, custard. Bao: Likes custard, kebab. Dislikes toast. Hui: Likes sorbet, shawarma, kebab. Ru: Likes soup, kebab. Dislikes shawarma. Tia: Likes kebab, pizza. Dislikes sorbet. Pat: Likes risotto. Dislikes muffins, sorbet. Riya: Likes custard. Dislikes toast, soup. Joe: Dislikes doughnut, risotto, toast. Nic: Dislikes custard, kebab, pizza. Liz: Likes soup, doughnut. Dislikes custard. Mina: Likes risotto, muffins. Dislikes pizza. Ansh: Likes custard. Dislikes sorbet, toast. Tim: Likes soup. Dislikes kebab, pizza. Sue: Likes pizza, soup. Dislikes custard. Chen: Likes doughnut, kebab. Dislikes muffins. Lia: Likes risotto. Dislikes kebab, shawarma. ', 'menu_items': ['kebab', 'toast', 'doughnut', 'risotto', 'soup', 'pizza', 'muffins', 'custard', 'sorbet', 'shawarma'], 'gpt_out': "Let's analyze the preferences to satisfy the conditions required to create the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists.\n\nFirst, let's see if there are any universally disliked items, as these could easily go into the 'not_orderable' list without affecting anyone's satisfaction. Sorbet and custard are disliked by multiple people, but no single food item is disliked by everyone. Meanwhile, shawarma, kebab, doughnut, toast, pizza, soup, and muffins appear on both liked and disliked lists, so we can't simply classify them without further analysis.\n\nWe should look for a food item that is liked by someone who dislikes nothing else that another one likes. For instance, Veer likes kebab and no one dislikes it, but Bao, Hui, Ru, Tia, and Chen also like kebab and their dislikes clash with others' preferences (shawarma, toast, sorbet).\n\nHere's a strategy to solve the problem:\n1. List out each person's likes and dislikes.\n2. Identify items that are exclusively liked or disliked to simplify the choice.\n3. Resolve conflicts step by step, ensuring we meet everyone's satisfaction criteria.\n\nFrom the list provided, we see that each person has at least one food item they like that isn't unanimously disliked, so a combination that satisfies all is feasible.\n\nNow let's find a pair of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists without clashes. We start with the most restricted individuals, who have the least flexibility due to the number of items they dislike that no one else likes. Anil dislikes risotto, shawarma, and custard, but no one else dislikes risotto and it's liked by Pat and Lia so we can put it in 'orderable'. This means Anil is satisfied and we don't need to worry about shawarma and custard for now.\n\nLet's add to the 'orderable' list:\n- Orderable: risotto\n\nWe will proceed to satisfy as many people as possible without creating clashes:\n\n- Veer likes kebab and doughnut. Since no one dislikes both of these foods, we can choose one for 'orderable'. We will assign doughnut for the moment as it's also liked by Vic and Liz.\n- Doughnut also appears in the like list of Chen, and since nobody dislikes it, we can confidently add it to the 'orderable' list.\n- Joe dislikes doughnut, but since doughnut is now in 'orderable', Joe is satisfied by having it on 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow with doughnut in 'orderable', we satisfy Veer, Vic, Liz, and Chen. Doughnut going into 'orderable' also satisfies Eli and Joe because they dislike it.\n\n- Orderable: risotto, doughnut\n- Not orderable: []\n\nNext, we'll add pizza to 'orderable' to satisfy Eli, since doughnut is not an option for him anymore. Ansh dislikes pizza, but we can put custard in 'not_orderable' to satisfy Ansh. Now Eli and Ansh are satisfied.\n\nHowever, suiting Eli creates a conflict because Mina, Nic, and Tim dislike pizza and we yet don't know what to serve them to make them satisfied. \n\nInstead of pizza, which is problematic, let's try to satisfy Eli with muffins, which he also likes. But Ken and Pat dislike muffins, which is again problematic.\n\nWe see that the previous steps start to generate conflicts, it's time to try another approach.\n\nLet's look again for a safe choice for everyone:\n- Veer: Likes kebab, doughnut. Both these items are problematic because kebab is disliked by Bao, and doughnut by Joe and Eli.\n- Ken: Likes shawarma, which is disliked by Ru and Lia.\n- Vic: Likes doughnut (problematic) and toast. Toast is disliked by Bao, Joe, and Ansh.\n- Eli: Likes pizza and muffins; both are problematic due to the dislikes.\n- Anil: Is satisfied with risotto already included in 'orderable'.\n- Bao likes custard and kebab, but both are disliked by multiple people.\n- Hui likes sorbet, shawarma, kebab, but sorbet is problematic due to the multiple dislikes.\n- Ru: Likes soup, kebab; kebab is problematic, we need to look at soup. Soup is disliked by Riya and Tim which is problematic.\n- Lia: Likes risotto which is already included in 'orderable'.\n- Joe: We have satisfied him with the exclusion of doughnut, which is already in 'orderable'.\n\nSo, let's adopt a more systematic approach by sorting the individuals based on the fewest likes to those with the most. Then we will try to start satisfying individuals starting from those with the fewest likes since they have less flexibility.\n\n- Anil and Lia are already satisfied with risotto.\n- Pat has just one like, which is risotto, so Pat is satisfied.\n- Tim likes only soup, which is problematic because it is disliked by Riya and Ansh.\n- Bao likes custard and kebab; kebab could be placed in 'orderable' if we manage not to serve custard. Toast, shawarma, and sorbet are disliked by Bao, but if we can satisfy others without these, it's workable.\n- Tia likes kebab and pizza and dislikes sorbet; we need to avoid sorbet and can aim to serve either kebab or pizza. If kebab goes to 'orderable', Bao is also satisfied.\n- Ru likes soup and kebab, and doesn't like shawarma. If kebab goes into 'orderable', Ru and Tia are satisfied without affecting Bao.\n- Hui likes sorbet, shawarma, kebab; sorbet is problematic due to dislikes, but shawarma is only disliked by Ru, who can be satisfied with kebab. So we can consider shawarma for 'orderable', satisfying Hui, or kebab if we can manage without shawarma.\n- Joe dislikes doughnut, risotto, toast, but since doughnut is already in 'orderable', Joe is satisfied.\n- Riya likes custard, dislikes toast, soup; we need to avoid these or put them in 'not_orderable' and can satisfy Riya with custard not being offered (in 'not_orderable').\n- Sue likes pizza, soup, and dislikes custard; we need to avoid custard.\n- Chen likes doughnut, kebab; dislikes muffins; since doughnut is already in 'orderable', Chen is satisfied.\n- Sue also likes pizza and soup (problematic due to Tim's dislike); custard is undesirable for her, and it's a common dislike with Ansh, hence can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Nic, dislikes custard, kebab, pizza, too many dislikes cause conflicts.\n\nSo now, let's try to satisfy people without conflicting with what has been satisfied already.\n\nPutting custard into 'not_orderable' would satisfy:\n\n- Vic,\n- Riya,\n- Ansh, and\n- Sue,\n\nIt also doesn't cause conflict for Eli who dislikes doughnut because the doughnut is already in 'orderable'. \n\nNow let's add kebab to 'orderable'. This would satisfy:\n\n- Veer (kebab),\n- Ru (kebab), without causing conflict because Ru dislikes shawarma, which is not being served yet.\n- Tia can be satisfied with kebab, avoiding the sorbet.\n\nThis leaves us with a few individuals left to satisfy:\n\n- Bao is satisfied with kebab in 'orderable'.\n- Hui can be served shawarma, as no one else besides Ru (already satisfied) dislikes it.\n- Chen was satisfied with doughnut earlier.\n- Nic's dislikes are all covered, as kebab, pizza, and custard are not conflicting with his preferences (kebab is in 'orderable', pizza hasn't been served, custard is in 'not_orderable').\n\nNow, everyone's preferences seem to have been addressed without any noted conflicts. Let's confirm the specifications.\n\nOrder:\n- risotto,\n- doughnut, and\n- kebab are orderable,\n\nNot order:\n- custard is not orderable.\n\nKen, Mina, and Liz have not been explicitly mentioned since they can be satisfied with shawarma and soup respectively.\n\nThus, the final lists without causing a conflict is:\n\n```python\norderable=['risotto', 'doughnut', 'kebab', 'shawarma', 'soup'], not_orderable=['custard']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 464, 'num_completion_tokens': 1785}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[1, 2, -3], [1, 3, -4], [-4, -1, -2], [-3, -2, -1], [3, 2, 1], [-2, -3, 1], [3, 4, -2], [1, 3, 2], [-4, -3, -2], [-3, 4, -2], [3, -4, 1], [4, -3, 2], [4, 2, -3], [1, 3, 4], [3, 2, -4], [-2, 3, -4], [1, 3, 4], [2, -1, 4], [-2, 1, 3], [4, -1, -2], [1, -3, -2], [4, 1, -2], [3, 1, 4], [-3, -2, 1]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Jaya: Likes hummus, roti. Dislikes tart. Raj: Likes hummus, tart. Dislikes quiche. Ravi: Dislikes quiche, hummus, roti. Ivy: Dislikes tart, roti, hummus. Yan: Likes tart, roti, hummus. Eva: Likes hummus. Dislikes roti, tart. Du: Likes tart, quiche. Dislikes roti. Bea: Likes hummus, tart, roti. Val: Dislikes quiche, tart, roti. Xan: Likes quiche. Dislikes tart, roti. Ty: Likes tart, hummus. Dislikes quiche. Tia: Likes quiche, roti. Dislikes tart. Pia: Likes quiche, roti. Dislikes tart. Lei: Likes hummus, tart, quiche. Ron: Likes tart, roti. Dislikes quiche. Tara: Likes tart. Dislikes roti, quiche. Kit: Likes hummus, tart, quiche. Zan: Likes roti, quiche. Dislikes hummus. Ray: Likes hummus, tart. Dislikes roti. Meg: Likes quiche. Dislikes hummus, roti. Liv: Likes hummus. Dislikes tart, roti. Dan: Likes quiche, hummus. Dislikes roti. Chen: Likes tart, hummus, quiche. Cy: Likes hummus. Dislikes tart, roti. ', 'menu_items': ['hummus', 'roti', 'tart', 'quiche'], 'gpt_out': "Let's first compile a list of all the likes and dislikes, and then see if we can find an 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' list that will satisfy everyone. We can do this by looking for conflicts in the lists of likes and dislikes.\n\nInitial Lists:\n- Likes: hummus (Jaya, Raj, Yan, Eva, Bea, Ty, Lei, Kit, Ray, Liv, Cy, Dan, Chen), roti (Jaya, Yan, Bea, Ron, Tia, Pia, Zan), tart (Raj, Yan, Du, Bea, Ty, Lei, Tara, Kit, Ray, Chen), quiche (Du, Xan, Tia, Pia, Lei, Kit, Meg, Dan, Chen, Zan)\n- Dislikes: tart (Jaya, Ivy, Eva, Val, Xan, Tia, Pia), roti (Ravi, Ivy, Eva, Du, Val, Ty, Tia, Pia, Ray, Liv, Meg, Dan, Cy), hummus (Ravi, Ivy, Zan, Meg), quiche (Raj, Ravi, Val, Ron, Tara)\n\nNext, we must look for any insurmountable conflicts – situations where one person's like is another's dislike. There are three key conflicts here:\n- Some like hummus (hummus is in the likes list), but some dislike it (hummus is in the dislikes list).\n- Some like roti, but some dislike it.\n- Some like tart, but some dislike it.\n- Some like quiche, but some dislike it.\n\nFor individuals satisfied with having at least one like in 'orderable' or one dislike in 'not_orderable', we can choose to have either hummus or quiche in 'orderable' since the number of individuals liking hummus is greater than any other single food item. If hummus is put in 'orderable', the people who dislike it must have another dislike in 'not_orderable' or a like in 'orderable' to be satisfied. Same with quiche.\n\nHowever, Ravi dislikes quiche, hummus, and roti. Ravi's satisfaction would mean that either quiche, hummus or roti must be in the 'not_orderable' list. Ivy also dislikes tart, roti and hummus, so to satisfy both Ravi and Ivy, quiche or hummus has to be in 'not_orderable' but this makes it impossible to satisfy those who only like quiche (Xan, Meg, Dan, Zan) or only like hummus (Eva, Liv, Cy).\n\nGiven this conflict, we cannot find a combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that satisfies all participants. As a result, the only solution is to output empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'.\n\nFinal output:\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 501, 'num_completion_tokens': 611}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 20, 'formula': [[3, -2, 1], [-4, 3, -1], [-1, -3, -4], [2, -3, -1], [-4, 1, -3], [-2, -1, -4], [-3, -4, 2], [1, 2, 4], [3, -4, 2], [-2, -4, -3], [3, -4, -1], [-2, -3, -1], [1, -2, -3], [4, 3, 2], [-2, -4, -1], [3, 2, -4], [4, -1, 2], [-2, 1, -4], [-1, 3, 2], [2, 4, 1]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Ian: Likes cheese, mousse. Dislikes korma. Shan: Likes cheese. Dislikes peach, mousse. Gus: Dislikes mousse, cheese, peach. Isha: Likes korma. Dislikes cheese, mousse. Tom: Likes mousse. Dislikes peach, cheese. Rohan: Dislikes korma, mousse, peach. Mel: Likes korma. Dislikes cheese, peach. Ava: Likes mousse, korma, peach. Riya: Likes cheese, korma. Dislikes peach. Ray: Dislikes korma, peach, cheese. Abe: Likes cheese. Dislikes peach, mousse. Jaya: Dislikes korma, cheese, mousse. Jun: Likes mousse. Dislikes korma, cheese. Arun: Likes peach, cheese, korma. Amy: Dislikes korma, peach, mousse. Sue: Likes cheese, korma. Dislikes peach. Lei: Likes peach, korma. Dislikes mousse. Sky: Likes mousse. Dislikes korma, peach. Tia: Likes cheese, korma. Dislikes mousse. Joe: Likes korma, peach, mousse. ', 'menu_items': ['mousse', 'korma', 'cheese', 'peach'], 'gpt_out': 'To solve this problem, we must create two lists: \'orderable\' (items that can be ordered) and \'not_orderable\' (items that cannot be ordered), such that everyone in the group is satisfied based on their individual likes and dislikes. First, let\'s list out all the unique food items mentioned:\n\n- Cheese\n- Mousse\n- Korma\n- Peach\n\nNow let’s identify potential conflicts — where an item being orderable would satisfy some individuals but dissatisfy others based on their likes and dislikes:\n\n1. Cheese is liked by many and disliked by some. If we add cheese to \'orderable\', individuals who dislike cheese could be dissatisfied. On the other hand, there are many that dislike cheese but like other items that could go into \'not_orderable\' to satisfy them.\n2. Mousse is also liked by many and disliked by some. This is similar to cheese, and adding it to \'orderable\' could leave some individuals dissatisfied.\n3. Korma is liked by a few and disliked by many. However, individuals who like korma do not all have a common dislike that could be put in \'not_orderable\'.\n4. Peach is liked by a few and disliked by many. The same argument as for korma applies here.\n\nGiven the satisfaction criteria and the food preferences, we must aim to satisfy everyone:\n\n- Gus, Rohan, Ray, Jaya, and Amy dislike all items, so they can only be satisfied by having items they dislike in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n- Mousse is disliked by the most number of people (7 individuals), which could suggest placing it in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Cheese is liked by the largest number of people and disliked by fewer, suggesting placing it in \'orderable.\'\n- Korma is disliked by 6 (vs liked by 6) people and is a contentious item like peach.\n- Peach is disliked by 11 and liked by 5 people, suggesting it might be placed in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nLet us try placing mousse in \'not_orderable\' and cheese in \'orderable\' and see if we can satisfy everyone:\n\n- "Orderable" list: cheese\n- "Not_orderable" list: mousse\n\nLet\'s examine if all are satisfied with cheese being orderable and mousse not_orderable:\n\n- Ian: satisfied (likes cheese)\n- Shan: satisfied (dislikes mousse)\n- Gus: satisfied (dislikes mousse)\n- Isha: not satisfied (both liked and disliked items are not in the correct lists)\n- Tom: satisfied (likes mousse, which cannot be ordered, but cheese can be)\n- Rohan: satisfied (dislikes mousse)\n- Mel: not satisfied (same reason as Isha)\n- Ava: satisfied (mousse cannot be ordered, which is one of the dislikes)\n- Riya: satisfied (likes cheese)\n- Ray: satisfied (dislikes mousse)\n- Abe: satisfied (dislikes mousse)\n- Jaya: satisfied (dislikes mousse)\n- Jun: satisfied (likes mousse, cannot be ordered)\n- Arun: not satisfied (likes cheese, which can be ordered, but also likes peach and korma, which must be in one of the lists to satisfy)\n- Amy: satisfied (dislikes mousse)\n- Sue: satisfied (likes cheese)\n- Lei: not satisfied (dislikes mousse, which cannot be ordered)\n- Sky: satisfied (likes mousse, cannot be ordered)\n- Tia: satisfied (likes cheese)\n- Joe: not satisfied (likes mousse, cannot be ordered)\n\nIsha, Mel, Arun, Lei, and Joe are not currently satisfied. We cannot satisfy them simultaneously with the current combination because both their likes and dislikes have not been met. Let us continue iterating:\n\nIf we put korma in \'not_orderable\', then Isha, Mel, and Joe will be satisfied, but Arun and Lei will still be dissatisfied. The individuals who dislike korma (Arun and Lei) also like peach, so if we put peach in \'orderable\', then they would also be satisfied.\n\n- "Orderable" list: cheese, peach\n- "Not_orderable" list: mousse, korma\n\nNow let\'s reassess everyone\'s satisfaction with peach being orderable and korma not_orderable:\n\n- Ian: satisfied (likes cheese)\n- Shan: satisfied (dislikes mousse)\n- Gus: satisfied (dislikes cheese, but satisfied since mousse is not orderable)\n- Isha: satisfied (dislikes mousse, is not orderable)\n- Tom: satisfied (likes mousse, which cannot be ordered, but cheese is orderable)\n- Rohan: satisfied (dislikes mousse, which is not orderable)\n- Mel: satisfied (dislikes peach, is orderable)\n- Ava: satisfied (dislikes korma, which is not orderable)\n- Riya: satisfied (likes cheese, which is orderable)\n- Ray: satisfied (dislikes mousse, which is not orderable)\n- Abe: satisfied (dislikes mousse, which is not orderable)\n- Jaya: satisfied (dislikes mousse, which is not orderable)\n- Jun: satisfied (likes mousse, which cannot be ordered)\n- Arun: satisfied (likes peach, which is orderable)\n- Amy: satisfied (dislikes mousse, which is not orderable)\n- Sue: satisfied (likes cheese, which is orderable)\n- Lei: satisfied (likes peach, which is orderable)\n- Sky: satisfied (likes mousse, which is not orderable)\n- Tia: satisfied (likes cheese, which is orderable)\n- Joe: satisfied (dislikes korma, which is not orderable)\n\nWe have found a combination where everyone is satisfied:\n\n- Everyone liking cheese is satisfied because cheese is in \'orderable\'.\n- Everyone disliking mousse is satisfied because mousse is in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Everyone liking peach is satisfied because peach is in \'orderable\'.\n- Everyone disliking korma is satisfied because korma is in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nFinal solution:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'cheese\', \'peach\'], not_orderable=[\'mousse\', \'korma\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 454, 'num_completion_tokens': 1312}
