_orderable\' to immediately satisfy those who dislike the items. For example, tiramisu is disliked by Ansh, Pia, Lin, Joy, Ken, Lan, Sky, and Ivy, so it is a good candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n\n2. Find a common like that we can put into \'orderable\' to satisfy a majority without conflicting with dislikes, for example, kebab is liked by Liz, Lin, Tia, May, Eli, Ian, and Wei (we should check if it does not conflict with anyone\'s dislikes).\n\nLet\'s proceed with these steps:\n\nAdd tiramisu to \'not_orderable\' to satisfy those who dislike it:\n- not_orderable = [tiramisu]\n\nLet\'s check if kebab satisfies the largest number without any conflicts:\n\n- Likes: Liz, Lin, Tia, May, Eli, Ian, Wei.\n- Dislikes: Qi, Ned, Xan, May, Ian.\n\nThere is a conflict; kebab is disliked by Qi, Ned, Xan, May, and Ian, so it can\'t be added to \'orderable\'. Let\'s find another item.\n\nNext, we can try adding cheese to \'orderable\', as several people like it (Riya, Tia, Zac, Neil, and Raj), but we need to check for conflicts:\n\n- Likes: Riya, Tia, Zac, Neil, Raj.\n- Dislikes: Ansh, Anil, Bo, Ivy.\n\nCheese also has conflicts, so we cannot add it to \'orderable\'. Continuing this process with other likes:\n\n- Cheese: Conflicts.\n- Cake: Conflicts (Joy, Anil, Cal dislike it).\n- Caramel: Conflicts (Liz, Pia, Tia, Zac, Xan, Neil, Raj, Cal, Bo dislike it).\n- Cheesecake: Conflicts.\n- Kebab: Conflicts.\n- Sushi: Conflicts.\n- Ravioli: Conflicts (Liz, Raj, Anil, Cal dislike it).\n\nWith no single item that can be added to \'orderable\' without conflict, let\'s try to satisfy individual participants by adding their dislikes to \'not_orderable\' and make sure to check for likes afterwards.\n\nBy adding tiramisu to \'not_orderable\', we\'ve satisfied multiple participants with one item because of their common dislike. We need to do the same thing to orderable.\n\nLet\'s add caramel to \'not_orderable\':\n- not_orderable = [tiramisu, caramel]\n\nThis addition seems to satisfy a number of participants who dislike caramel. Now we need to find at least one item that can go to \'orderable\' for the others.\n\nLet\'s check "cheese":\n- Likes cheese: Riya, Tia, Zac, Neil, Raj (no existing conflict in \'not_orderable\').\n\nLet\'s add cheese to \'orderable\':\n- orderable = [cheese]\n\nSo far, no conflicts arise, as those who dislike cheese have tiramisu or caramel in \'not_orderable\'. We continue this process for the rest:\n\n- Cake is liked by Riya, Anil, Ned, but disliked by Joy and Cal, so it can\'t be added.\n- Sushi is liked by Sky, Xan, Pia, Lin, Zac, Neil, but disliked by Lin, Eli, Ian, Bo, so it can\'t be added.\n- Kebab is liked by a lot, but disliked by Ned, Xan, Ken, May, Ian; may be conflicting.\n- Ravioli is liked by Joy, Wei but disliked by several, can\'t be added.\n\nConsidering multiple candidates likes and dislikes, it is difficult to find an item to add to \'orderable\' without conflict, so we must try combinations that do not cause conflicts.\n\nAfter trying various combinations, we see that it\'s impossible to create an \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' list that satisfies all participants without conflict. Therefore, the solution is:\n- orderable = []\n- not_orderable = []\n\nIn conclusion, we would output:\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 498, 'num_completion_tokens': 950}
