ere are a few individuals who dislike it, meaning we can't place it in either list right away.\n- Vindaloo is another item that is both liked and disliked by different individuals, so we must be careful.\n- Toast, naan, custard, and sushi also appear on both like and dislike lists.\n\nSince there's no single item that is universally disliked, let's identify at least one item that each individual likes that does not appear on someone else's dislike list:\n\n- Sushi is liked by several and only disliked by a few, but we can't satisfy everyone with it alone.\n- Naan is also liked by some and disliked by others.\n- Toast cannot be on the orderable list as it has multiple dislikes.\n- Custard has many likes and dislikes, making it challenging to satisfy everyone.\n- Lobster is liked by several people but disliked as well.\n\nLooking at the preferences, we need to find at least one item for each person that is not countered by a dislike from someone else. This is challenging because for almost every liked item there is at least one person who dislikes it.\n\nNext, look for the least common disliked food item(s) that is not liked by anyone or liked by the least number of people which is lobsters and maybe custard. \n\nIf we put lobster in 'not_orderable', we satisfy Kit, Ava, Liz, Jim, Jaya, Veer, Pat, Kai, Ying, Chen, Raj, Cy, Ty, Leo, Naina, Ada, Rina, Lei, Vic, Kim, Sam, Yue, Tim, Geet, and Ron without contradicting anyone else's dislikes. That's 25 people just with one item in 'not_orderable'.\n\nIf we put custard in 'not_orderable', we satisfy Cal, Liz, Pat, Ty, Zara, Ru, Rob, Mina, Xiu, Ansh, Tim. That's 11 people.\n\nBy placing lobster in the 'not_orderable' list and custard in the 'not_orderable', we can satisfy a large number of individuals. But, we still have a number of people left to satisfy, let's see if there are any common likes that haven't been contradicted by dislikes:\n\n- Espresso is liked by a vast majority. Lobster eliminates some of the dislikes for espresso. So we have the potential to put espresso in 'orderable'.\n- Pizza is also universally liked. It is possible that by placing an item that is commonly disliked (like lobster) in 'not_orderable', and by placing a commonly liked item (like espresso) in 'orderable', we could satisfy the majority.\n\nLet's assume we place lobster and custard in 'not_orderable' and espresso in 'orderable':\n\n- We have already determined lobster in 'not_orderable' satisfies many individuals.\n- Espresso in 'orderable' would satisfy: Cal, Ava, Liz, Jim, Jaya, Veer, Kai, Min, Zara, Sia, Naina, Max, Tao, Ping, Riya, Neil, Mei, Ada, Vic, Lin, Kim, Zan, and several others, as they all like espresso and its presence in 'orderable' would satisfy their criterion for satisfaction without contradicting anyone's dislikes because lobster dislikers are already satisfied by the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nAt this point, we have satisfied the largest group of people with just three items across two lists. The only individuals left to consider are those who dislike both lobster and espresso, which we can check against the remaining likes to find additional items for the 'orderable' list if necessary.\n\nAfter satisfying this large group with the inclusion of espresso in 'orderable', we can look for the second most-liked item that does not appear on the dislikes of those who still need to be satisfied.\n\nAdding naan to 'orderable' because it satisfies Fay, Liz, Chen, Raj, Lou, Min, Tara, Rina, Oli, Liv, Bea, Ansh, Sky, Sam, Ron, Wes, and Ken since many people dislike toast.\n\nWe've satisfied nearly everyone; our next step is to look at individuals who haven't been included to see if they can be satisfied by adding or avoiding adding certain items to the lists.\n\nNow, Let's tabulate if anyone is unsatisfied if we put lobster and custard in 'not_orderable', and espresso and naan in 'orderable'. Then we can cross-reference and check for any conflicts.\n\nParticipants satisfied with 'orderable': Cal, Ava, Fay, Liz, Jim, Jaya, Veer, Kai, Min, Zara, Sia, Naina, Max, Tao, Ping, Riya, Neil, Mei, Ada, Vic, Lin, Kim, Zan, Lou, Tara, Rina, Oli, Liv, Bea, Ansh, Sky, Ron, Geet\n\nParticipants satisfied with 'not_orderable': Kit, Pat, Ying, Chen, Raj, Cy, Ty, Leo\n\nParticipants satisfied with either: Everyone else, since they like either espresso or naan and have their disliked food in 'not_orderable'.\n\nIt looks like we've found a selection that satisfies everyone. Our final 'orderable' list includes at least one liked item for everyone, and our 'not_orderable' list includes at least one disliked item for everyone, with no item appearing on both lists. Each individual is satisfied.\n\nSo the final solution can be outputted as:\n\n```python\norderable=['espresso', 'naan'], not_orderable=['lobster', 'custard']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1267, 'num_completion_tokens': 1349}
