n, taking into account that the remaining items should fulfill the condition for every person.\n\nLet\'s list the food items and the number of likes/dislikes for each:\n\n**Likes:**\n- couscous: 6\n- tandoori: 12\n- guacamole: 4\n- roti: 5\n- espresso: 6\n  \n**Dislikes:**\n- guacamole: 7\n- tandoori: 6\n- roti: 6\n- couscous: 5\n- espresso: 4\n\nFirstly, let\'s look for conflicts. We will find items that are liked and disliked by different individuals. A major conflict would be if an item liked by someone is also disliked by someone else. We would want to minimize these conflicts so that the items can be easily classified into \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\'.\n\nConflicts are:\n- couscous: liked by 6, disliked by 5\n- tandoori: liked by 12, disliked by 6\n- guacamole: liked by 4, disliked by 7\n- roti: liked by 5, disliked by 6\n- espresso: liked by 6, disliked by 4\n\nTo resolve these conflicts, we will look for items that can be placed in \'orderable\' that are not disliked by many people. The item that stands out is "guacamole", with only 4 likes and the highest dislikes (7). So, we could potentially put "guacamole" on the \'not_orderable\' list. \n\nBut we also need to make sure that there\'s at least one item on the \'orderable\' list that each person who likes guacamole can also enjoy. Looking at the list, we observe:\n\n- Aarav (likes tandoori), \n- Shan (likes espresso), \n- Rina (likes tandoori), \n- Lee (likes tandoori, espresso).\n\nAll of these individuals also like either "espresso" or "tandoori", and these items are liked by others as well. We have a larger number of likes for "tandoori" (12 likes), but it\'s also disliked by 6 people. On the other hand, "espresso" is liked by 6 and disliked by fewer people (4). To avoid making large groups unhappy, we prefer placing "espresso" in the \'orderable\' list, as it will satisfy individuals who like "guacamole" without making a large unhappy group.\n\nNow, let\'s put "guacamole" in the \'not_orderable\'