y based on unanimous likes or dislikes.\n\nSince all items are conflicted, we'll try to use logical reasoning to satisfy everyone’s criteria.\n\nWe need to make sure that for the people who like burgers, there has to be another item that they also like which isn't universally disliked by another participant. A similar approach must be taken for each conflicted item. By placing one liked item of each person on the 'orderable' list that is not disliked by anyone, or one disliked item of each person on the 'not_orderable' list that is not liked by anyone else, we can ensure satisfaction for everyone.\n\nLooking closer, we find that there is a challenge with satisfying both sides of any conflict because of the members who only like/dislike items involved in conflicts:\n\nFor instance, members who only like burger (Sky, Ana, Fay, Amy, Mei) but dislike non-conflicted items, create a condition where a burger must be on the 'orderable' list. However, that directly conflicts with members who dislike burgers but like conflicted items (Du, May, Aarav, Lia, Ru, Gen, Yue, Kit, Val, Tao, Eva).\n\nThis creates an unsolvable situation where we cannot satisfy both groups, as placing burgers in 'orderable' will unsatisfy the burger-dislikers and vice-versa.\n\nGiven this information and based on the satisfaction criteria provided, there is no combination possible that would satisfy all participants. Therefore, the output should be empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable':\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1195, 'num_completion_tokens': 712}
